{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789789537,"nonce":"vWbYTiDEyqaijlVH0JLMuON+iDc=","title":"LLVM: a bitcast of an address to an aggregate held by reference panicked","message":"The boxes pass (`boxes::expand`, Read::Data of an aggregate) and the Python\nfrontend's placeholder value both say \"the aggregate at this address\" as\n`Cast { Bitcast, ptr -\u003e Struct }`. Cranelift and the interpreter hold a\nmulti-field struct as its address, so the cast is a no-op there. The LLVM\nbackend built an LLVM `bitcast ptr to %struct` and panicked at the next\nuse of the value as a pointer (`into_pointer_value` in Load), in the\npromoter thread: `Found StructValue(...) but expected PointerValue`.\n\nEvery promotion of a function that reads a list out of a box was lost this\nway; with typed tuples in nbody that was `__main__` and `advance`, which\nhad become promotable.\n\nFix: in the Cast arm, a Bitcast whose target `is_held_by_reference` and\nwhose operand is a pointer or an integer yields the address (inttoptr for\nan integer). The wider convention question is e1efccb / e756498.","files":null}]}